# 0. Simple web stack

## The flow

A user opens their browser and types www.foobar.com.

1. The browser needs an IP address, not a name, so it asks DNS to resolve
   www.foobar.com. The www record for foobar.com is an A record pointing to
   8.8.8.8, so DNS answers 8.8.8.8.
2. The browser opens a TCP connection to 8.8.8.8 on port 80 (HTTP) and sends
   an HTTP GET request.
3. Nginx, the web server listening on that port, receives the request. If the
   request is for a static file (an image, a CSS file) it serves it directly.
   If the request needs logic, it forwards the request to the application
   server.
4. The application server runs the application files (the code base), executes
   the business logic, and asks the MySQL database for whatever data it needs.
5. MySQL answers with the data, the application builds the HTML page, hands it
   back to Nginx, and Nginx sends it to the browser as an HTTP response. The
   browser renders the page.

## Specifics

**What is a server**
A server is a machine - physical or virtual - that runs software which waits
for requests from clients over a network and answers them. It is not the
software itself; it is the machine hosting it.

**What is the role of the domain name**
A domain name is a human-readable label that maps to an IP address. People
remember foobar.com; they do not remember 8.8.8.8. It also means the IP can
change without anyone having to learn a new address.

**What type of DNS record www is in www.foobar.com**
It is an A record (an IPv4 address record) pointing to 8.8.8.8. It could also
be a CNAME pointing to another name, but here the requirement is a record
pointing to the server IP, so it is an A record.

**What is the role of the web server**
Nginx accepts incoming HTTP(S) connections, serves static content directly,
handles concurrency and keep-alive connections, and reverse-proxies the
dynamic requests to the application server.

**What is the role of the application server**
It runs the application code. It executes the business logic, talks to the
database, renders the templates, and returns the generated page to the web
server. The web server deals with the HTTP protocol; the application server
deals with the program.

**What is the role of the database**
MySQL stores the application's data persistently - users, posts, orders - and
answers the queries the application sends it.

**What is the server using to communicate with the computer of the user**
The TCP/IP protocol stack. IP routes the packets to 8.8.8.8, TCP provides a
reliable ordered connection on port 80, and HTTP is the application protocol
carried inside that TCP connection.

## Issues with this infrastructure

**SPOF (Single Point Of Failure)**
Everything lives on one server. If that machine dies - hardware failure, a
crashed Nginx, a full disk, a network outage - the entire website is down.
There is no second anything.

**Downtime when maintenance is needed**
Deploying new code usually requires restarting the web server or the
application server. During that restart the site is unreachable. The same is
true for any OS upgrade or configuration change.

**Cannot scale if there is too much incoming traffic**
One machine has a fixed amount of CPU, RAM, and bandwidth. Past a certain
number of concurrent users it saturates, requests queue up, and the site slows
to a crawl or stops answering. The only option is a bigger machine (vertical
scaling), which has a hard ceiling and still requires downtime.
